A||||5||Ronnie||Timing chain replacement||||6||
Z||000000||Ronnie||12-18-2000||09:00 PM||||Do you have to drop the cradle to replace the timing chain? Or can it be done without dropping it?||||reg||6
Z||000001||Ronnie||12-18-2000||09:56 PM||||need an answer fella's, please help me out here.||||reg||6
Z||000002||StuGood||12-18-2000||10:17 PM||||I did it on mine w/o dropping the cradle (1986 SE 2.8-l V6).  You <B>do</B> have to remove the RR tire and inner fender liner.  I also left the water pump attached to the front cover, to avoid extra work/ gaskets.  <P>It was a fairly tight squeeze, but very do-able.||||reg||14
Z||000003||David Gamache||12-19-2000||05:24 PM||||I agree, very do able with the engine in place on the V-6. I have done 2 GT's so far and in both cases the chain was fairly easy once the wheel and splash shields were removed.  This is usually a good time to replace the harmonic balancer as well. The 4 cyc is the pain. the engine has to be lowered to slip out the cam to have the gear pressed off. A puller on the 4cyc usually just tears apart the nylon gear unless you are very lucky, which I never am.||||reg||12
Z||000004||Ronnie||12-19-2000||05:45 PM||||Thanks, that's what I wanted to know.||||reg||6
Z||000005||2birds||12-19-2000||06:52 PM||||To add to what David said above, the balancer will at least need to be sleeved (about $8 for a Fel-pro kit), or your timing cover will start to leak oil, since the balancer probably has a groove worn in it.  It only took me 8 years to figure out that this was the source of my belt squeal problem.  The chain and gear work isn't much harder than doing the water pump, especially if you already have a gear puller.  Have fun.||||reg||1
